{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "f7def645",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# *p-value* estimation\n",
    "\n",
    "In this notebook I test empyrically my theoretical function for the estimation of the p-value, given the observed frequency of successful tests. In particular, when $0$ successes over $n$ tests are observed, my formula provides an expected value for the p-value of\n",
    "\n",
    "$E[p]=\\frac{1}{n+2}$\n",
    "\n",
    "differently from the one proposed in *Phipson et al. (2010)*, which provides a value of\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\frac{1}{n+1}$"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "10923964",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Uniform prior\n",
    "We assume a uniform prior for the value of the \"true p-value\". Any number in $[0, 1]$ is equally likely."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "id": "cf7078b8",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import random\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "\n",
    "def choose_a_true_p(lower=0, upper=1):\n",
    "    ''' Returns a random value of the \"true p-value\" to be used. '''\n",
    "    return random.uniform(lower, upper)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5f107564",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Samples are random\n",
    "We can generate a sample of size $n$, where successes are produced with a probability equal to a chosen p-value, using the following function. Each element in the sample has a probability equal to *true_p* to be a success."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "id": "5ca066cd",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def get_sample(n, true_p):\n",
    "    ''' Returns a sample of size n, where each element has a probability true_p\n",
    "    of being True, and a probability 1-true_p of being False.\n",
    "    true_p stands for \"true p-value\". '''\n",
    "    sample = []\n",
    "    for i in range(n):\n",
    "        if random.random() < true_p:\n",
    "            sample.append(True)\n",
    "        else:\n",
    "            sample.append(False)\n",
    "    return sample"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5f02c4df",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We are interested in knowing when a sample doesn't cointain any successes. indeed, we are going to collect examples of sets of size $n$ with $0$ successes."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "id": "8da37056",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def is_0_frequency(sample):\n",
    "    ''' Returns True if all the elements in the sample are False.\n",
    "    Returns False otherwise. '''\n",
    "    return not sum(sample)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "87d97598",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Estimating the average p-value\n",
    "My formula estimates the expected value of the *p-value*, given a uniform prior, and given that $0$ out $n$ tests were successes.\n",
    "\n",
    "The following function provides an approximation by performing many \"experiments\". Each experiment consists of choosing randomly a value for the \"true p-value\", and then generating a sample of size $n$, based on that p-value. Whenever a p-value leads to a sample of $0/n$ successes, its value is recorded. The function returns the average value of the recorded p-values, which corresponds to the expected value of a p-value known to have generated $0/n$ successes."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "id": "3dbc548a",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def empyrical_average_true_p(n, n_experiments):\n",
    "    ''' Performs as many experiments as specified by n_experiments.\n",
    "    Each experiment consists of choosing randomly a value for the\n",
    "    \"true p-value\", and then generating a sample of size n, based on that\n",
    "    p-value. Whenever a p-value leads to a sample of 0/n successes, its value\n",
    "    is recorded. The function returns the average value of the recorded\n",
    "    p-values, which corresponds to the expected value of a p-value known to\n",
    "    have generated 0/n successes. '''\n",
    "    true_p_list = []\n",
    "    for i in range(n_experiments):\n",
    "        true_p = choose_a_true_p()\n",
    "        a_sample = get_sample(n, true_p)\n",
    "        if is_0_frequency(a_sample):\n",
    "            true_p_list.append(true_p)\n",
    "    return sum(true_p_list)/len(true_p_list)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8872997f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Test\n",
    "Here I run a test, chosing $n=10$.\n",
    "\n",
    "The computational approximation provided by the *empyrical_average_true_p* function depends on the number of experiments performed: the *n_experiments* parameter. We will study the convergence of the approximation for increasing values of *n_experiments*. # The quality of the approximation will depend on the number of experiments performed. The less accurate estimation will be obtained by performing $10^{SMALLEST\\_EXP}$ experiments, while the most accurate will be obtained by perfomring $10^{LARGEST\\_EXP}$ experiments."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 5,
   "id": "c9871634",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "n = 10\n",
    "\n",
    "SMALLEST_EXP = 3\n",
    "LARGEST_EXP = 8"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3525f6c5",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The two p-value estimators to be compared are the following:\n",
    "\n",
    "My formula: $\\frac{1}{n+2}$\n",
    "\n",
    "Phipson et al. (2010): $\\frac{1}{n+1}$"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 6,
   "id": "80d824bc",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "theo_elia = 1/(n+2)\n",
    "theo_phipson = 1/(n+1)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c8b36321",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We can run all the study and collect results in a table format, to make the comparison easy."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 7,
   "id": "5a49f2be",
   "metadata": {
    "scrolled": true
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Estimating E[p-value] with 10^3 experiments ...\n",
      "Estimating E[p-value] with 10^4 experiments ...\n",
      "Estimating E[p-value] with 10^5 experiments ...\n",
      "Estimating E[p-value] with 10^6 experiments ...\n",
      "Estimating E[p-value] with 10^7 experiments ...\n",
      "Estimating E[p-value] with 10^8 experiments ...\n",
      "\n",
      "RESULTS TABLE\n",
      "-------------\n",
      "    n  n_experiments  empyrical   1/(n+2)   1/(n+1)  error_elia  error_phip\n",
      "0  10           1000   0.079364  0.083333  0.090909    0.003969    0.011545\n",
      "1  10          10000   0.086007  0.083333  0.090909    0.002674    0.004902\n",
      "2  10         100000   0.084159  0.083333  0.090909    0.000826    0.006750\n",
      "3  10        1000000   0.083552  0.083333  0.090909    0.000219    0.007357\n",
      "4  10       10000000   0.083265  0.083333  0.090909    0.000069    0.007645\n",
      "5  10      100000000   0.083305  0.083333  0.090909    0.000028    0.007604\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "n_experiments_list = []\n",
    "empyrical_list = []\n",
    "error_elia_list = []\n",
    "error_phip_list = []\n",
    "\n",
    "for exponent in range(SMALLEST_EXP, LARGEST_EXP+1):\n",
    "    n_experiments = 10**exponent\n",
    "    print(\"Estimating E[p-value] with 10^{} experiments ...\".format(exponent))\n",
    "    \n",
    "    emp = empyrical_average_true_p(n, n_experiments)\n",
    "    err_elia = abs(emp - theo_elia)\n",
    "    err_phip = abs(emp - theo_phipson)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    n_experiments_list.append(n_experiments)\n",
    "    empyrical_list.append(emp)\n",
    "    error_elia_list.append(err_elia)\n",
    "    error_phip_list.append(err_phip)\n",
    "\n",
    "len_table = len(range(SMALLEST_EXP, LARGEST_EXP+1))\n",
    "res_table = pd.DataFrame({\"n\": [n] * len_table,\n",
    "                          \"n_experiments\": n_experiments_list,\n",
    "                          \"empyrical\": empyrical_list,\n",
    "                          \"1/(n+2)\": [theo_elia] * len_table,\n",
    "                          \"1/(n+1)\": [theo_phipson] * len_table,\n",
    "                          \"error_elia\": error_elia_list,\n",
    "                          \"error_phip\": error_phip_list})\n",
    "\n",
    "print('\\nRESULTS TABLE\\n-------------')\n",
    "print(res_table)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "fc451c84",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The table shows that the computational approximation of $E[p]$ approaches $\\frac{1}{n+2}$. Indeed, using my formula the error approaches $0$, while using Phipson's formula the error approaches the difference between my formula and their formula, $\\frac{1}{n+1} - \\frac{1}{n+2}$, which is"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 8,
   "id": "b0c62d19",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "0.007575757575757583\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "expected_offset = theo_phipson - theo_elia\n",
    "print(expected_offset)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "da603aca",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "(this is the error you would expect when using Phipson's formula if $E[p]$ is not $\\frac{1}{n+1}$, but $\\frac{1}{n+2}$)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7a2a66bb",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We can also see the convergence using line plots."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 9,
   "id": "78cba21c",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "\n",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 432x288 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {
      "needs_background": "light"
     },
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "x = list(range(SMALLEST_EXP, LARGEST_EXP+1))\n",
    "plt.title('Comparison of errors')\n",
    "plt.xticks(x)\n",
    "plt.xlabel(r'$\\log_{10}(n_{experiments})$')\n",
    "plt.ylabel('error')\n",
    "plt.plot(x, res_table['error_elia'], label='$theo_E$')\n",
    "plt.plot(x, res_table['error_phip'], label='$theo_P$')\n",
    "plt.axhline(y=(theo_phipson - theo_elia),\n",
    "            c='r', linestyle='dashed', label='expected offset')\n",
    "plt.ylim(bottom=0)\n",
    "plt.legend()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1bcf4b91",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "I'm going to assume that this doesn't mean that they made a mistake. For some reason, they argue that $\\frac{1}{n+1}$ is an ideal estimator in some context. To me, it is equal to $p(m=0)$ in my formula, so it doesn't really makes sense as an estimator of the p-value (we are not interested in the probability of seeing $0$ successes, but rather in the value of the p-value *given* that we have observed $0$ successes. But other things can be numerically equal to $\\frac{1}{n+1}$, which could be an ideal estimator of the p-value. I guess I'll have to take the time to read their paper and see what is precisely that they are saying."
   ]
  }
 ],
 "metadata": {
  "kernelspec": {
   "display_name": "Python 3 (ipykernel)",
   "language": "python",
   "name": "python3"
  },
  "language_info": {
